<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-limit-laws">
<title>Limit Laws</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Functions, algebraic manipulation, trigonometric functions,
exponential and logarithmic functions, sequences, inequalities,
and basic calculus notation.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this reference section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>interpret the notation for finite and infinite limits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish two-sided and one-sided limits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use constant, identity, sum, difference, product, quotient,
          power, and root laws;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>evaluate polynomial and rational-function limits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand when direct substitution is valid;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize indeterminate forms;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>simplify removable discontinuities algebraically;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use conjugates in radical limits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use fundamental trigonometric limits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>apply the squeeze theorem;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand limits of compositions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize continuity-based limit evaluation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>evaluate limits at infinity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compare polynomial growth rates;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compare logarithmic, polynomial, exponential, and factorial growth;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize horizontal asymptotes;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish infinite limits from limits at infinity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use dominant-term analysis;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret sequence limits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use monotone and bounded sequence principles conceptually;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize asymptotic equivalence;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use Big-O and little-o notation conceptually;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize common special limits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand the connection between limits and continuity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>avoid common errors involving infinity and indeterminate forms.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-meaning-of-limit-reference">
<title>Meaning of a Limit</title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L
}</md>
</p>

<p>is read:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{the limit as }x\text{ approaches }a
\text{ of }f(x)\text{ is }L.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Informally, this means that <m>f(x)</m> can be made arbitrarily close to <m>L</m>
by taking <m>x</m> sufficiently close to <m>a</m>, without requiring <m>x=a</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-epsilon-delta-limit-reference">
<title>Formal Epsilon--Delta Definition</title>
<p>The statement</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L
}</md>
</p>

<p>means that for every</p>

<p>
<md>\varepsilon&gt;0,</md>
</p>

<p>there exists</p>

<p>
<md>\delta&gt;0</md>
</p>

<p>such that</p>

<p>
<md>0&lt;|x-a|&lt;\delta</md>
</p>

<p>implies</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|f(x)-L|&lt;\varepsilon.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The symbols are read:</p>

<p>
<md>\varepsilon
=
\text{epsilon},</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\delta
=
\text{delta}.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-two-sided-limit-reference">
<title>Two-Sided Limits</title>
<p>A two-sided limit requires agreement from both directions.</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L
}</md>
</p>

<p>exists if and only if</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a^-}f(x)
=
\lim_{x\to a^+}f(x)
=
L.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-left-hand-limit-reference">
<title>Left-Hand Limits</title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a^-}f(x)
}</md>
</p>

<p>means that <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m> from values less than <m>a</m>.</p>

<p>The superscript minus indicates approach from the left.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-right-hand-limit-reference">
<title>Right-Hand Limits</title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a^+}f(x)
}</md>
</p>

<p>means that <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m> from values greater than <m>a</m>.</p>

<p>The superscript plus indicates approach from the right.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-two-sided-limit-dne">
<title>When a Two-Sided Limit Does Not Exist</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a^-}f(x)
\neq
\lim_{x\to a^+}f(x),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}f(x)
\text{ does not exist}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This commonly occurs at jump discontinuities.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-constant-limit-law">
<title>Constant Law</title>
<p>For constant <m>c</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}c
=
c.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-identity-limit-law">
<title>Identity Law</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}x
=
a.
}</md>
</p>

<p>More generally,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}x^n
=
a^n
}</md>
</p>

<p>for positive integers <m>n</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-constant-multiple-limit-law">
<title>Constant Multiple Law</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L,</md>
</p>

<p>then for constant <m>c</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
cf(x)
=
cL.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-sum-limit-law">
<title>Sum Law</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}g(x)=M,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
[f(x)+g(x)]
=
L+M.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-difference-limit-law">
<title>Difference Law</title>
<p>Under the same assumptions,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
[f(x)-g(x)]
=
L-M.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-product-limit-law">
<title>Product Law</title>
<p>If both limits exist,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
f(x)g(x)
=
LM.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-quotient-limit-law">
<title>Quotient Law</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}g(x)=M,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>M\neq0,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}
=
\frac{L}{M}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-power-limit-law">
<title>Power Law</title>
<p>For positive integer <m>n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
[f(x)]^n
=
L^n
}</md>
</p>

<p>whenever</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-root-limit-law">
<title>Root Law</title>
<p>For suitable domains,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
\sqrt[n]{f(x)}
=
\sqrt[n]{L}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>If <m>n</m> is even, the relevant quantities must remain in the real domain.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-absolute-value-limit-law">
<title>Absolute Value Law</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}|f(x)|
=
|L|.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-polynomial-limit-reference">
<title>Polynomial Limits</title>
<p>For a polynomial</p>

<p>
<md>p(x),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}p(x)
=
p(a).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus polynomial limits can be evaluated by direct substitution.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-polynomial-limit">
<title>Worked Example: Polynomial Limit</title>
<example>
<title>Direct Substitution</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to2}
(3x^2-5x+4).</md>
</p>

<p>Since polynomials are continuous,</p>

<p>
<md>3(2)^2-5(2)+4
=
12-10+4
=
6.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to2}
(3x^2-5x+4)
=
6.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-rational-function-limit-reference">
<title>Rational Function Limits</title>
<p>For rational function</p>

<p>
<md>R(x)
=
\frac{p(x)}{q(x)},</md>
</p>

<p>if</p>

<p>
<md>q(a)\neq0,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}
=
\frac{p(a)}{q(a)}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-direct-substitution-limit">
<title>Direct Substitution</title>
<p>Direct substitution is appropriate when the function is continuous at the
point of interest.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}f(x)
=
f(a)
}</md>
</p>

<p>whenever <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-indeterminate-forms-reference">
<title>Indeterminate Forms</title>
<p>Expressions such as</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac00,
\qquad
\frac{\infty}{\infty},
\qquad
0\cdot\infty,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\infty-\infty,
\qquad
0^0,
\qquad
1^\infty,
\qquad
\infty^0
}</md>
</p>

<p>are called indeterminate forms.</p>

<p>They are not answers.</p>

<p>They indicate that further analysis is required.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-zero-over-zero">
<title>The Form <m>0/0</m></title>
<p>If direct substitution gives</p>

<p>
<md>\frac00,</md>
</p>

<p>possible methods include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>factoring;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>rationalizing;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>simplifying algebraically;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>trigonometric identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>fundamental limits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>L'Hospital's Rule when its hypotheses are satisfied;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>series expansions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-factoring-limit-method">
<title>Factoring Method</title>
<p>A removable factor can often be canceled before evaluating the limit.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{x^2-a^2}{x-a}
=
x+a</md>
</p>

<p>for</p>

<p>
<md>x\neq a.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
\frac{x^2-a^2}{x-a}
=
2a.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-factoring-limit">
<title>Worked Example: Factoring</title>
<example>
<title>Resolve <m>0/0</m> by Factoring</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to3}
\frac{x^2-9}{x-3}.</md>
</p>

<p>Factor:</p>

<p>
<md>x^2-9
=
(x-3)(x+3).</md>
</p>

<p>For <m>x\neq3</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{x^2-9}{x-3}
=
x+3.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to3}
\frac{x^2-9}{x-3}
=
6.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-rationalization-limit-method">
<title>Rationalization Method</title>
<p>When radicals produce <m>0/0</m>, multiply by a conjugate.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
\sqrt{x}-\sqrt a
}{
x-a
}</md>
</p>

<p>can be multiplied by</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
\sqrt{x}+\sqrt a
}{
\sqrt{x}+\sqrt a
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{
\sqrt{x}-\sqrt a
}{
x-a
}
=
\frac{
1
}{
\sqrt{x}+\sqrt a
}
}</md>
</p>

<p>for <m>x\neq a</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-radical-limit">
<title>Worked Example: Radical Limit</title>
<example>
<title>Use a Conjugate</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to4}
\frac{
\sqrt{x}-2
}{
x-4
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Multiply by the conjugate:</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
\sqrt{x}-2
}{
x-4
}
\frac{
\sqrt{x}+2
}{
\sqrt{x}+2
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
x-4
}{
(x-4)(\sqrt{x}+2)
}
=
\frac{
1
}{
\sqrt{x}+2
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to4}
\frac{
\sqrt{x}-2
}{
x-4
}
=
\frac14.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-piecewise-limit-reference">
<title>Piecewise-Function Limits</title>
<p>For a piecewise function, evaluate left- and right-hand limits separately.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)
=
\begin{cases}
x+1, &amp; x&lt;2,\\
x^2, &amp; x\geq2.
\end{cases}</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to2^-}f(x)=3,</md>
</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to2^+}f(x)=4.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to2}f(x)
\text{ does not exist}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-squeeze-theorem-reference">
<title>The Squeeze Theorem</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>g(x)
\leq
f(x)
\leq
h(x)</md>
</p>

<p>near <m>a</m>, and</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}g(x)
=
\lim_{x\to a}h(x)
=
L.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}f(x)
=
L.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-squeeze-theorem">
<title>Worked Example: Squeeze Theorem</title>
<example>
<title>Oscillatory Function</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}
x^2\sin\left(\frac1x\right).</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>-1
\leq
\sin\left(\frac1x\right)
\leq
1,</md>
</p>

<p>multiplying by <m>x^2\geq0</m> gives</p>

<p>
<md>-x^2
\leq
x^2\sin\left(\frac1x\right)
\leq
x^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}(-x^2)=0</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}x^2=0,</md>
</p>

<p>the squeeze theorem gives</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
x^2\sin\left(\frac1x\right)
=
0.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-fundamental-sine-limit">
<title>Fundamental Sine Limit</title>
<p>When angles are measured in radians,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\sin x}{x}
=
1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is one of the fundamental limits of calculus.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-related-sine-limits">
<title>Related Sine Limits</title>
<p>For constant <m>a</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\sin(ax)}{x}
=
a.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\sin(ax)}{bx}
=
\frac{a}{b}
}</md>
</p>

<p>when <m>b\neq0</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-trigonometric-limit">
<title>Worked Example: Trigonometric Limit</title>
<example>
<title>Scaled Sine Limit</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\sin5x}{x}.</md>
</p>

<p>Write</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{\sin5x}{x}
=
5
\frac{\sin5x}{5x}.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\sin5x}{5x}
=
1.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\sin5x}{x}
=
5.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-tangent-fundamental-limit">
<title>Tangent Fundamental Limit</title>
<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>\tan x
=
\frac{\sin x}{\cos x},</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\tan x}{x}
=
1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-cosine-fundamental-limit">
<title>Cosine Fundamental Limit</title>
<p>A standard result is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{1-\cos x}{x^2}
=
\frac12.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Equivalently,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
1-\cos x
\sim
\frac{x^2}{2}
}</md>
</p>

<p>as <m>x\to0</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-cosine-limit">
<title>Worked Example: Cosine Limit</title>
<example>
<title>Scaled Cosine Limit</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{
1-\cos3x
}{
x^2
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Write</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
1-\cos3x
}{
x^2
}
=
9
\frac{
1-\cos3x
}{
(3x)^2
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\begin{answerbox}
\[
\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{
1-\cos3x
}{
x^2
}
=
\frac92.
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{answerbox}</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-exponential-fundamental-limit">
<title>Exponential Fundamental Limit</title>
<p>A standard limit is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{e^x-1}{x}
=
1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>More generally,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{e^{ax}-1}{x}
=
a.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-logarithmic-fundamental-limit">
<title>Logarithmic Fundamental Limit</title>
<p>A related logarithmic limit is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\ln(1+x)}{x}
=
1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>More generally,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\ln(1+x)
\sim
x
}</md>
</p>

<p>as <m>x\to0</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-e-limit-reference">
<title>Definition of <m>e</m> by a Limit</title>
<p>One defining limit is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
e
=
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\left(
1+\frac1n
\right)^n.
}</md>
</p>

<p>A continuous-variable version is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
(1+x)^{1/x}
=
e.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-general-exponential-type-limit">
<title>General Exponential-Type Limit</title>
<p>For suitable real <m>a</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
(1+ax)^{1/x}
=
e^a.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\left(
1+\frac{a}{n}
\right)^n
=
e^a.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-composition-limit-law">
<title>Composition Law for Limits</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}g(x)=L</md>
</p>

<p>and <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>L</m>.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
f(g(x))
=
f(L).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This justifies direct substitution into continuous outer functions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-continuity-limit-reference">
<title>Continuity and Limits</title>
<p>A function <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>a</m> when</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}f(x)
=
f(a).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This requires:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><m>f(a)</m> is defined;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)</m> exists;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>the limit equals the function value.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-common-continuous-functions-reference">
<title>Continuous Functions Commonly Used</title>
<p>The following are continuous on their natural domains:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>polynomials;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>rational functions where the denominator is nonzero;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>exponential functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>logarithmic functions on positive arguments;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>sine and cosine;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>tangent where cosine is nonzero;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>root functions on their real domains;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>inverse trigonometric functions on their domains.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-limits-at-infinity-reference">
<title>Limits at Infinity</title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\infty}f(x)=L
}</md>
</p>

<p>describes the behavior of <m>f(x)</m> as <m>x</m> grows without bound.</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to-\infty}f(x)=L
}</md>
</p>

<p>describes behavior as <m>x</m> becomes arbitrarily negative.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-horizontal-asymptote-limit">
<title>Horizontal Asymptotes</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\infty}f(x)=L
}</md>
</p>

<p>or</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to-\infty}f(x)=L,
}</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y=L
}</md>
</p>

<p>is a horizontal asymptote in the corresponding direction.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-basic-reciprocal-infinity-limits">
<title>Basic Reciprocal Limits</title>
<p>For positive integer <m>n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\infty}
\frac{1}{x^n}
=
0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to-\infty}
\frac{1}{x^n}
=
0.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-polynomial-dominant-term">
<title>Polynomial Dominant Terms</title>
<p>For polynomial</p>

<p>
<md>p(x)
=
a_nx^n+\cdots+a_0,</md>
</p>

<p>the highest-degree term determines the dominant growth as</p>

<p>
<md>|x|\to\infty.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
p(x)
\sim
a_nx^n.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-polynomial-infinity">
<title>Worked Example: Polynomial at Infinity</title>
<example>
<title>Dominant Term</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to\infty}
(3x^4-2x+7).</md>
</p>

<p>The dominant term is</p>

<p>
<md>3x^4.</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>3x^4\to\infty,</md>
</p>

<p>we have</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\infty}
(3x^4-2x+7)
=
\infty.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-rational-functions-infinity">
<title>Rational Functions at Infinity</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
a_nx^n+\cdots
}{
b_mx^m+\cdots
}.</md>
</p>

<p>The behavior depends on the degrees <m>n</m> and <m>m</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-rational-degree-less">
<title>Numerator Degree Less Than Denominator</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>n&lt;m,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\pm\infty}
\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}
=
0.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-rational-equal-degree">
<title>Equal Degrees</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>n=m,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\pm\infty}
\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}
=
\frac{a_n}{b_n}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the limit is the ratio of leading coefficients.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-rational-degree-greater">
<title>Numerator Degree Greater Than Denominator</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>n&gt;m,</md>
</p>

<p>then the rational function generally grows without bound in magnitude or
behaves like a polynomial of degree <m>n-m</m>.</p>

<p>Polynomial long division can reveal the asymptotic behavior.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-rational-infinity">
<title>Worked Example: Rational Limit at Infinity</title>
<example>
<title>Equal Degrees</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to\infty}
\frac{
5x^3-2x
}{
2x^3+7
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Divide numerator and denominator by <m>x^3</m>:</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
5-\frac{2}{x^2}
}{
2+\frac{7}{x^3}
}.</md>
</p>

<p>As <m>x\to\infty</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{2}{x^2}\to0,
\qquad
\frac{7}{x^3}\to0.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\infty}
\frac{
5x^3-2x
}{
2x^3+7
}
=
\frac52.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-radicals-at-infinity">
<title>Radicals at Infinity</title>
<p>When factoring powers from radicals, remember:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sqrt{x^2}=|x|.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt{x^2+1}
=
|x|
\sqrt{
1+\frac1{x^2}
}.</md>
</p>

<p>As <m>x\to+\infty</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>|x|=x.</md>
</p>

<p>As <m>x\to-\infty</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>|x|=-x.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-radical-infinity">
<title>Worked Example: Radical at Infinity</title>
<example>
<title>Factor Correctly From a Square Root</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to-\infty}
\frac{
\sqrt{x^2+4}
}{
x
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Factor <m>x^2</m> from the radical:</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt{x^2+4}
=
|x|
\sqrt{
1+\frac4{x^2}
}.</md>
</p>

<p>For <m>x\to-\infty</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>|x|=-x.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
\sqrt{x^2+4}
}{
x
}
=
-
\sqrt{
1+\frac4{x^2}
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Hence</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to-\infty}
\frac{
\sqrt{x^2+4}
}{
x
}
=
-1.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-infinite-limits-reference">
<title>Infinite Limits</title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}f(x)
=
\infty
}</md>
</p>

<p>means <m>f(x)</m> grows arbitrarily large as <m>x\to a</m>.</p>

<p>This does not mean that infinity is an ordinary real-number output.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-vertical-asymptote-limit">
<title>Vertical Asymptotes</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a^-}f(x)
=
\pm\infty</md>
</p>

<p>or</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a^+}f(x)
=
\pm\infty,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x=a
}</md>
</p>

<p>is typically a vertical asymptote.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-reciprocal-infinite-limit">
<title>Basic Reciprocal Infinite Limit</title>
<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0^+}
\frac1x
=
+\infty,
}</md>
</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0^-}
\frac1x
=
-\infty.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the two-sided limit</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}\frac1x</md>
</p>

<p>does not exist as a single infinite limit.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-even-reciprocal-infinite-limit">
<title>Even Reciprocal Powers</title>
<p>For even positive integer <m>n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac1{x^n}
=
+\infty.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Both sides approach positive infinity.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-odd-reciprocal-infinite-limit">
<title>Odd Reciprocal Powers</title>
<p>For odd positive integer <m>n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0^+}
\frac1{x^n}
=
+\infty,
}</md>
</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0^-}
\frac1{x^n}
=
-\infty.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-infinity-not-number">
<title>Infinity Is Not a Number in Ordinary Limit Algebra</title>
<p>One should not manipulate infinity as though it were an ordinary real number.</p>

<p>Expressions such as</p>

<p>
<md>\infty-\infty</md>
</p>

<p>or</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</md>
</p>

<p>are not defined by ordinary arithmetic.</p>

<p>They are indeterminate forms describing competing growth.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-growth-hierarchy-reference">
<title>Growth Hierarchy</title>
<p>As <m>x\to\infty</m>, a standard growth hierarchy is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\log x
\ll
x^\alpha
\ll
a^x
}</md>
</p>

<p>for</p>

<p>
<md>\alpha&gt;0,
\qquad
a&gt;1.</md>
</p>

<p>For sequences, one often extends this to</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\log n
\ll
n^\alpha
\ll
a^n
\ll
n!
\ll
n^n.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-logarithm-versus-power">
<title>Logarithms Versus Powers</title>
<p>For every <m>\alpha&gt;0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\infty}
\frac{\ln x}{x^\alpha}
=
0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus every positive power eventually grows faster than the logarithm.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-power-versus-exponential">
<title>Powers Versus Exponentials</title>
<p>For <m>a&gt;1</m> and every positive integer <m>n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\infty}
\frac{x^n}{a^x}
=
0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus exponential growth eventually dominates polynomial growth.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-exponential-versus-factorial">
<title>Exponentials Versus Factorials</title>
<p>For every fixed <m>a&gt;0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\frac{a^n}{n!}
=
0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus factorial growth dominates any fixed-base exponential.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-sequence-limits-reference">
<title>Sequence Limits</title>
<p>A sequence</p>

<p>
<md>(a_n)</md>
</p>

<p>converges to <m>L</m> if</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{n\to\infty}a_n=L.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Formally, for every</p>

<p>
<md>\varepsilon&gt;0,</md>
</p>

<p>there exists <m>N</m> such that</p>

<p>
<md>n\geq N</md>
</p>

<p>implies</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|a_n-L|&lt;\varepsilon.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-basic-sequence-limit-examples">
<title>Basic Sequence Examples</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\frac1n
=
0,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\frac1{n^p}
=
0
\qquad
(p&gt;0),
}</md>
</p>

<p>and if</p>

<p>
<md>|r|&lt;1,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{n\to\infty}
r^n
=
0.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-geometric-sequence-behavior">
<title>Geometric Sequence Behavior</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>a_n=r^n,</md>
</p>

<p>the behavior depends on <m>r</m>.</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>|r|&lt;1,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>a_n\to0.</md>
</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>r=1,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>a_n=1.</md>
</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>r&gt;1,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>a_n\to\infty.</md>
</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>r=-1,</md>
</p>

<p>the sequence oscillates and does not converge.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-monotone-convergence-sequence">
<title>Monotone Convergence Principle</title>
<p>A fundamental result for real sequences is:</p>

<p>\begin{importantbox}
Every monotone increasing sequence that is bounded above converges, and every
monotone decreasing sequence that is bounded below converges.
\end{importantbox}</p>

<p>This is a consequence of completeness of <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-bounded-not-convergent">
<title>Boundedness Alone Does Not Guarantee Convergence</title>
<p>The sequence</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
a_n=(-1)^n
}</md>
</p>

<p>is bounded because</p>

<p>
<md>-1\leq a_n\leq1,</md>
</p>

<p>but it does not converge.</p>

<p>Thus boundedness alone is insufficient.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-subsequence-limit-principle">
<title>Subsequence Principle</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>a_n\to L,</md>
</p>

<p>then every subsequence also converges to <m>L</m>.</p>

<p>Therefore, if two subsequences converge to different limits, the original
sequence cannot converge.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-oscillatory-sequence">
<title>Worked Example: Oscillatory Sequence</title>
<example>
<title>Show a Sequence Diverges</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>a_n=(-1)^n.</md>
</p>

<p>The even subsequence satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>a_{2n}=1.</md>
</p>

<p>The odd subsequence satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>a_{2n+1}=-1.</md>
</p>

<p>Since the two subsequences have different limits,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
(-1)^n
\text{ does not converge}.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-recursive-sequence-limits">
<title>Recursive Sequence Limits</title>
<p>If a recursive sequence</p>

<p>
<md>a_{n+1}=F(a_n)</md>
</p>

<p>converges to <m>L</m>, and <m>F</m> is continuous at <m>L</m>, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L=F(L).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This fixed-point equation gives candidate limits.</p>

<p>It does not by itself prove convergence.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-recursive-limit">
<title>Worked Example: Recursive Limit Candidate</title>
<example>
<title>Find a Possible Limit</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>a_{n+1}
=
\sqrt{2+a_n}</md>
</p>

<p>and assume the sequence converges to <m>L\geq0</m>.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>L
=
\sqrt{2+L}.</md>
</p>

<p>Squaring gives</p>

<p>
<md>L^2-L-2=0.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>(L-2)(L+1)=0.</md>
</p>

<p>Since <m>L\geq0</m>,</p>

<p>This calculation identifies the possible limit; a complete proof must also
establish convergence.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
L=2.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-asymptotic-equivalence-limit">
<title>Asymptotic Equivalence</title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x)\sim g(x)
}</md>
</p>

<p>as <m>x\to a</m> means</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}
\to1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This says that <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> have the same leading-order behavior.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-common-local-equivalences">
<title>Common Local Equivalences</title>
<p>As <m>x\to0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin x
\sim
x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan x
\sim
x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
e^x-1
\sim
x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\ln(1+x)
\sim
x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
1-\cos x
\sim
\frac{x^2}{2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-using-asymptotic-equivalence">
<title>Using Asymptotic Equivalence</title>
<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{
\sin3x
}{
\ln(1+5x)
}</md>
</p>

<p>can be analyzed using</p>

<p>
<md>\sin3x\sim3x</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\ln(1+5x)\sim5x.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{
\sin3x
}{
\ln(1+5x)
}
=
\frac35.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-big-o-limit-reference">
<title>Big-O Notation</title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x)=O(g(x))
}</md>
</p>

<p>as <m>x\to a</m> means, roughly, that <m>|f(x)|</m> is bounded by a constant
multiple of <m>|g(x)|</m> near the limiting regime.</p>

<p>More precisely, there exists <m>C&gt;0</m> such that</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|f(x)|
\leq
C|g(x)|
}</md>
</p>

<p>sufficiently near the relevant limit.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-little-o-limit-reference">
<title>Little-o Notation</title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x)=o(g(x))
}</md>
</p>

<p>means</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}
\to0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus <m>f</m> is asymptotically negligible relative to <m>g</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-taylor-local-limit-reference">
<title>Taylor-Type Local Approximations</title>
<p>Near <m>x=0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
e^x
=
1+x+\frac{x^2}{2}
+
O(x^3),
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin x
=
x-\frac{x^3}{6}
+
O(x^5),
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos x
=
1-\frac{x^2}{2}
+
O(x^4),
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\ln(1+x)
=
x-\frac{x^2}{2}
+
O(x^3).
}</md>
</p>

<p>These expansions can evaluate complicated limits efficiently.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-series-limit">
<title>Worked Example: Series-Based Limit</title>
<example>
<title>Use Leading Terms</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{
e^x-1-x
}{
x^2
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Using</p>

<p>
<md>e^x
=
1+x+\frac{x^2}{2}
+
O(x^3),</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>e^x-1-x
=
\frac{x^2}{2}
+
O(x^3).</md>
</p>

<p>Divide by <m>x^2</m>:</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
e^x-1-x
}{
x^2
}
=
\frac12+O(x).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{
e^x-1-x
}{
x^2
}
=
\frac12.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-lhospital-reference">
<title>L'Hospital's Rule</title>
<p>Suppose a quotient produces an indeterminate form</p>

<p>
<md>\frac00</md>
</p>

<p>or</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{\pm\infty}{\pm\infty},</md>
</p>

<p>and the hypotheses of L'Hospital's Rule are satisfied.</p>

<p>Then under appropriate conditions,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}
=
\lim_{x\to a}
\frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}
}</md>
</p>

<p>provided the derivative quotient limit exists or is infinite in the required
sense.</p>

<p>This is not a general rule for arbitrary quotients.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-lhospital">
<title>Worked Example: L'Hospital's Rule</title>
<example>
<title>A <m>0/0</m> Limit</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{
e^x-1
}{
x
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Direct substitution gives</p>

<p>
<md>\frac00.</md>
</p>

<p>Differentiate numerator and denominator:</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{d}{dx}(e^x-1)=e^x,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{d}{dx}x=1.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{
e^x-1
}{
x
}
=
\lim_{x\to0}
e^x.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
1.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-repeated-lhospital">
<title>Repeated L'Hospital Applications</title>
<p>If the derivative quotient remains indeterminate and the hypotheses remain
valid, L'Hospital's Rule may sometimes be applied again.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
1-\cos x
}{
x^2
}</md>
</p>

<p>gives <m>0/0</m>.</p>

<p>One application gives</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
\sin x
}{
2x
},</md>
</p>

<p>which is still <m>0/0</m>.</p>

<p>A second application gives</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
\cos x
}{
2
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{
1-\cos x
}{
x^2
}
=
\frac12.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-zero-times-infinity">
<title>Transforming <m>0\cdot\infty</m></title>
<p>A product such as</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)g(x)</md>
</p>

<p>with</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)\to0</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>g(x)\to\infty</md>
</p>

<p>has indeterminate form</p>

<p>
<md>0\cdot\infty.</md>
</p>

<p>Rewrite as a quotient:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x)g(x)
=
\frac{f(x)}{1/g(x)}
}</md>
</p>

<p>or</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x)g(x)
=
\frac{g(x)}{1/f(x)}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-infinity-minus-infinity">
<title>Transforming <m>\infty-\infty</m></title>
<p>Expressions with form</p>

<p>
<md>\infty-\infty</md>
</p>

<p>often require:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>a common denominator;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>factoring dominant terms;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>rationalization;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>logarithmic combination;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>algebraic simplification.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-infinity-minus-infinity">
<title>Worked Example: Infinity Minus Infinity</title>
<example>
<title>Rationalize at Infinity</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to\infty}
\left(
\sqrt{x^2+x}-x
\right).</md>
</p>

<p>Multiply by the conjugate:</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt{x^2+x}-x
=
\frac{
x
}{
\sqrt{x^2+x}+x
}.</md>
</p>

<p>For <m>x&gt;0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\sqrt{x^2+x}
=
x
\sqrt{
1+\frac1x
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{x}{
x\sqrt{1+1/x}+x
}
=
\frac{
1
}{
\sqrt{1+1/x}+1
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Hence</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\infty}
\left(
\sqrt{x^2+x}-x
\right)
=
\frac12.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-indeterminate-powers-reference">
<title>Indeterminate Powers</title>
<p>The forms</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
0^0,
\qquad
1^\infty,
\qquad
\infty^0
}</md>
</p>

<p>are indeterminate.</p>

<p>A common method is logarithmic transformation.</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>y=f(x)^{g(x)},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\ln y
=
g(x)\ln f(x).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Analyze the logarithmic limit first, then exponentiate.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-worked-one-infinity">
<title>Worked Example: <m>1^\infty</m></title>
<example>
<title>An Exponential Limit</title>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to\infty}
\left(
1+\frac{2}{x}
\right)^x.</md>
</p>

<p>Use the standard limit</p>

<p>
<md>\left(
1+\frac{a}{x}
\right)^x
\to
e^a.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\infty}
\left(
1+\frac{2}{x}
\right)^x
=
e^2.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-ratio-comparison-limits">
<title>Limit Comparison Through Ratios</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}
=
1,
}</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x)\sim g(x).
}</md>
</p>

<p>If instead</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}
\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}
=
0,
}</md>
</p>

<p>then <m>f</m> is asymptotically smaller than <m>g</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-dominant-balance-reference">
<title>Dominant Balance</title>
<p>When several terms occur, the dominant terms often determine the limit.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
7x^5-3x^2+1
}{
2x^5+x
}</md>
</p>

<p>has leading terms</p>

<p>
<md>7x^5</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>2x^5.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{
7x^5-3x^2+1
}{
2x^5+x
}
\to
\frac72
}</md>
</p>

<p>as <m>|x|\to\infty</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-limits-and-inequalities">
<title>Limits and Inequalities</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)\leq g(x)</md>
</p>

<p>near <m>a</m>, and both limits exist.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}f(x)
\leq
\lim_{x\to a}g(x).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Order is preserved under limits.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-nonnegative-limit-rule">
<title>Nonnegativity and Limits</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)\geq0</md>
</p>

<p>near <m>a</m> and</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)\to L,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L\geq0.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-uniqueness-of-limits">
<title>Uniqueness of Limits</title>
<p>If a limit exists, it is unique.</p>

<p>A function cannot satisfy both</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=M</md>
</p>

<p>with</p>

<p>
<md>L\neq M.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-finite-limit-local-boundedness">
<title>Local Boundedness From a Finite Limit</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L</md>
</p>

<p>with finite <m>L</m>, then <m>f</m> is bounded in some punctured neighborhood of
<m>a</m>.</p>

<p>Thus finite convergence prevents arbitrarily large local oscillation near
the point.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-limit-independent-point-value">
<title>Limits Ignore the Function Value at the Point</title>
<p>The value</p>

<p>
<md>f(a)</md>
</p>

<p>does not directly determine</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x).</md>
</p>

<p>For example, a function may satisfy</p>

<p>
<md>f(a)=100</md>
</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=3.</md>
</p>

<p>Limits concern nearby behavior.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-removable-discontinuity-limit">
<title>Removable Discontinuity</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L</md>
</p>

<p>exists but either</p>

<p>
<md>f(a)</md>
</p>

<p>is undefined or</p>

<p>
<md>f(a)\neq L.</md>
</p>

<p>Then the discontinuity can be removed by defining</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(a)=L.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-jump-discontinuity-limit">
<title>Jump Discontinuity</title>
<p>A jump occurs when finite one-sided limits exist but disagree:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a^-}f(x)
\neq
\lim_{x\to a^+}f(x).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Then the two-sided limit does not exist.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-infinite-discontinuity-limit">
<title>Infinite Discontinuity</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)
\to
\pm\infty</md>
</p>

<p>from at least one side as <m>x\to a</m>, the function has an infinite
discontinuity at <m>a</m>.</p>

<p>A vertical asymptote often occurs.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-oscillatory-limit-failure">
<title>Oscillatory Failure of a Limit</title>
<p>The function</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin\left(\frac1x\right)
}</md>
</p>

<p>does not have a limit as</p>

<p>
<md>x\to0.</md>
</p>

<p>It oscillates infinitely often between <m>-1</m> and <m>1</m>.</p>

<p>However,</p>

<p>
<md>x\sin\left(\frac1x\right)
\to0</md>
</p>

<p>by the squeeze theorem.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-common-special-limits-table">
<title>Common Special Limits</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\sin x}{x}
=
1
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\tan x}{x}
=
1
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{1-\cos x}{x^2}
=
\frac12
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{e^x-1}{x}
=
1
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\ln(1+x)}{x}
=
1
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
(1+x)^{1/x}
=
e
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\left(
1+\frac1n
\right)^n
=
e
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-quick-limit-law-table">
<title>Quick Limit-Law Table</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)\to L,
\qquad
g(x)\to M,</md>
</p>

<p>then:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
c
\to
c
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
cf(x)
\to
cL
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x)+g(x)
\to
L+M
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x)-g(x)
\to
L-M
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f(x)g(x)
\to
LM
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}
\to
\frac{L}{M},
\qquad
M\neq0
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
[f(x)]^n
\to
L^n
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|f(x)|
\to
|L|.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-limit-laws-common-errors">
<title>Common Errors</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-treating-0-0-as-zero">
<title>Treating <m>0/0</m> as Zero</title>
<p>The expression</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac00
}</md>
</p>

<p>is indeterminate.</p>

<p>It does not equal zero.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-treating-as-one">
<title>Treating <m>\infty/\infty</m> as One</title>
<p>The form</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{\infty}{\infty}
}</md>
</p>

<p>does not imply a limit of <m>1</m>.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{x}{x^2}
\to0,</md>
</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{x^2}{x}
\to\infty.</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-treating-infinity-as-an-ordinary-number">
<title>Treating Infinity as an Ordinary Number</title>
<p>Do not use ordinary arithmetic rules blindly with <m>\infty</m>.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\infty-\infty</md>
</p>

<p>is indeterminate.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-the-function-value-determines-the-limit">
<title>Assuming the Function Value Determines the Limit</title>
<p>It is possible that</p>

<p>
<md>f(a)
\neq
\lim_{x\to a}f(x).</md>
</p>

<p>The function may even be undefined at <m>a</m> while the limit exists.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-ignoring-one-sided-limits">
<title>Ignoring One-Sided Limits</title>
<p>A two-sided limit exists only if the left and right limits agree.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-canceling-at-the-point-instead-of-near-the-point">
<title>Canceling at the Point Instead of Near the Point</title>
<p>When</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{(x-a)(x+b)}{x-a}</md>
</p>

<p>is simplified, cancellation is valid only for</p>

<p>
<md>x\neq a.</md>
</p>

<p>This is sufficient for the limit because limits use nearby values.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-x-2-x">
<title>Forgetting <m>\sqrt{x^2}=|x|</m></title>
<p>This error is especially serious for</p>

<p>
<md>x\to-\infty.</md>
</p>

<p>For negative <m>x</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>|x|=-x.</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-trigonometric-fundamental-limits-in-degrees">
<title>Using Trigonometric Fundamental Limits in Degrees</title>
<p>The standard formulas</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{\sin x}{x}\to1</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{1-\cos x}{x^2}\to\frac12</md>
</p>

<p>require <m>x</m> in radians.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-l-hospital-s-rule-on-a-nonindeterminate-form">
<title>Using L'Hospital's Rule on a Nonindeterminate Form</title>
<p>L'Hospital's Rule is designed for certain</p>

<p>
<md>0/0</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\infty/\infty</md>
</p>

<p>forms.</p>

<p>It should not be applied automatically to every quotient.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-a-fixed-point-equation-proves-sequence-convergence">
<title>Assuming a Fixed-Point Equation Proves Sequence Convergence</title>
<p>From</p>

<p>
<md>L=F(L)</md>
</p>

<p>one obtains only candidate limits.</p>

<p>One must independently prove the sequence converges.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-limit-at-infinity-with-infinite-limit">
<title>Confusing Limit at Infinity With Infinite Limit</title>
<p>The statement</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to\infty}f(x)=L
}</md>
</p>

<p>describes input tending to infinity.</p>

<p>The statement</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=\infty
}</md>
</p>

<p>describes output becoming unbounded.</p>

<p>These are different concepts.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-bounded-sequences-converge">
<title>Assuming Bounded Sequences Converge</title>
<p>The sequence</p>

<p>
<md>(-1)^n</md>
</p>

<p>is bounded but divergent.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-oscillation-always-prevents-a-product-limit">
<title>Assuming Oscillation Always Prevents a Product Limit</title>
<p>Although</p>

<p>
<md>\sin(1/x)</md>
</p>

<p>does not converge as <m>x\to0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>x\sin(1/x)
\to0.</md>
</p>

<p>A shrinking factor can dominate bounded oscillation.</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-limit-evaluation-workflow">
<title>Limit Evaluation Workflow</title>
<p>A practical order of operations is:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>identify the limiting point or direction;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>try direct substitution;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>if a finite value results, stop when continuity justifies it;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>if <m>0/0</m> occurs, factor or rationalize;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use trigonometric identities when needed;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use fundamental limits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>consider the squeeze theorem for bounded oscillation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>for limits at infinity, identify dominant terms;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>divide rational functions by the largest relevant power;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use <m>\sqrt{x^2}=|x|</m> when radicals are involved;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>transform other indeterminate forms algebraically;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use L'Hospital's Rule only when applicable;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use series or asymptotic equivalence for advanced limits;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>check one-sided behavior when signs or discontinuities matter.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-limit-laws-subsec-limit-laws-exercises">
<title>Exercises</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to3}(2x+5).</md>
